Setting. Let a distance space be a pair $(W,d)$ where $W$ is a non-empty set and $d:W\times W\to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ is a function (possibly subject to axioms such as $d(x,x)=0$, symmetry, identity of indiscernibles, and the triangle inequality $d(x,z)\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$; the special case in which all four axioms hold yields a metric space). A distance model is a triple $\mathfrak{M}=(W,d,\nu)$ where $\nu$ assigns to each propositional variable a subset of $W$.

The modal language MS for distances has formulas built from propositional variables and Boolean connectives together with families of parametrised modal operators indexed by positive rational (or real) numbers $a>0$:
- $\exists^{<a}\varphi$ (resp. $\exists^{\le a}\varphi$, $\exists^{>a}\varphi$, $\exists^{\ge a}\varphi$), interpreted at a point $x\in W$ as: there exists $y\in W$ with $d(x,y)<a$ (resp. $\le a$, $>a$, $\ge a$) such that $\mathfrak{M},y\models\varphi$;
- and the dual universal operators $\forall^{<a}$, $\forall^{\le a}$, etc.

A formula $\varphi$ is satisfiable over a class $\mathcal{C}$ of distance models if there exists $(W,d,\nu)\in\mathcal{C}$ and $x\in W$ with $\mathfrak{M},x\models\varphi$. By a fragment of MS we mean any syntactically defined subset of MS formulas (for instance, restricting the allowed comparison symbols $<,\le,>,\ge$, restricting which parameters $a$ may occur, or restricting nesting/Boolean combinations of distance modalities).

Encodings. The numerical parameters $a$ appearing in modalities are rational numbers given either in unary or in binary; the input size of a formula depends on this choice.

Problem. For each natural class $\mathcal{C}$ of distance models (e.g.\ all distance spaces; symmetric distance spaces; distance spaces satisfying the triangle inequality; metric spaces; metric spaces over a fixed scale such as $\mathbb{N}$, $\mathbb{Q}$, or $\mathbb{R}$):
(a) classify the fragments of MS according to the decidability and complexity of their satisfiability problem over $\mathcal{C}$, and according to whether the set of MS-validities over $\mathcal{C}$ admits a finite (or recursive) axiomatisation;
(b) determine whether there exist maximal decidable fragments of MS with respect to $\mathcal{C}$ — that is, fragments whose satisfiability problem is decidable but every proper syntactic extension (within naturally specified extension operations) yields an undecidable satisfiability problem;
(c) for each such maximal decidable fragment, determine the exact computational complexity of its satisfiability problem under unary encoding of the numerical parameters and under binary encoding of the numerical parameters.

